    "slug": "ai-incentives-in-new-healthcare-policy-what-it-means-for-rcm-and-practice-growth",
    "title": "AI Incentives in New Healthcare Policy: What It Means for RCM and Practice Growth",
    "date": "December 16, 2025",
    "featuredIn": "The Guardian",
    "featuredDate": "December 14, 2025",
    "tag": "AI & Technology",
    "sourceUrl": "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/dec/14/trump-healthcare-ai",
    "body": "A new healthcare policy direction is putting artificial intelligence at the center of federal investment strategy. According to reporting from The Guardian, recent policy proposals tie major funding incentives to healthcare technology adoption, including AI driven tools across clinical and operational workflows. The goal is to modernize care delivery, improve efficiency, and strengthen access, particularly in underserved and rural settings.\n\n#### What the Policy Signals\n\nThe policy links healthcare funding to the use of advanced technologies such as AI supported documentation, analytics, and automation. While positioned as a modernization effort, it also introduces expectations around how providers manage data, workflows, and compliance. Adoption is no longer just a competitive advantage. It is becoming a structural requirement tied to long term funding and growth.\n\n#### Operational and Revenue Cycle Impact\n\nFor provider organizations, AI adoption has direct implications for revenue cycle performance. When implemented thoughtfully, AI can help reduce denials by identifying documentation gaps earlier in the process. It can support coding accuracy by aligning clinical notes with payer requirements. It can also streamline documentation workflows, easing administrative burden for clinicians and staff while improving turnaround times for billing.\n\nHowever, technology alone is not enough. Without proper oversight, governance, and training, AI tools can introduce risk rather than reduce it. Practices must ensure that automation supports accuracy, compliance, and transparency rather than replacing critical human review.\n\n#### What Practices Should Prioritize\n\nAs AI becomes more tightly linked to healthcare policy and funding, practices should focus on secure implementation, clear governance frameworks, and workforce readiness. Training teams to work alongside AI tools is essential to realizing efficiency gains without compromising quality or compliance.\n\n#### The Takeaway\n\nAI incentives in healthcare policy highlight a shift toward technology driven operations. For practices, success will depend on how responsibly and strategically these tools are deployed, especially within revenue cycle workflows.\n\nCosentus helps provider organizations strengthen revenue cycle effectiveness through analytics, coding accuracy support, and operational optimization. By grounding advanced technology adoption in strong governance and proven workflows, practices can improve efficiency, protect revenue, and position themselves for sustainable growth in a rapidly evolving healthcare landscape."
